WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.140
Most onscreen versions of Superman and Batman
have looked a lot like they do in the original

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.990
DC Comics, but not all superheroes are created
equal.

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:13.380
Some of the most iconic superheroes have received
major makeovers when they jumped off the printed

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:14.380
page.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:17.840
Here’s how DC superheroes should really
look.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.080
Superman

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:23.460
There is no shortage of angry fans' gripes
about the DC Extended Universe version of

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.800
Superman...but his appearance probably isn't
one of them.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:29.930
Though it's not an exact replica of the comics
character's usual threads, Henry Cavill's

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:34.230
physical appearance for the role isn't one
of the DC Extended Universe's problems.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.530
Unless, that is, you really like red underpants.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:40.120
One of the most prominent distinctions of
the new Kal-El costume is the fact that, like

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:43.000
everything else about the modern take, it's
much darker.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.700
Whereas the Superman of old donned bright
blue tights with bright red accents and a

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.399
cape, the new suit is somewhere in the deep
denim family.

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:55.860
With a scaly texture woven throughout, Superman's
super-suit appears much rougher than the leotards

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:56.860
of Krypton's past.

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:00.610
And, of course, it's not hard to miss the
fact that Cavill's costume also ditches the

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:03.310
thick belt and outer britches featured in
the drawings.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:07.460
It makes sense, too...Superman's original
outer-underpants design was based on that

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.260
of circus strongmen of the late 19th and early
20th centuries.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:15.170
That's not really a thing anymore, so it’s
probably okay to drop the underoos...they

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.240
finally have in the comics, too.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:18.240
Batman

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:24.460
In 2016's super-punch-fest, Dawn of Justice,
Ben Affleck's Batman was most obviously inspired

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:29.560
by the Caped Crusader's journey in the 1986
comic miniseries The Dark Knight Returns.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:34.400
In the four-part Frank Miller epic, an older
Bruce returns from retirement to combat rampant

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:38.720
violence that has taken over Gotham City and
dukes it out with Superman along the way.

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.549
His costume, too, is clearly inspired by the
series.

00:01:41.549 --> 00:01:46.220
The slate gray color, shorter "ears" on the
cowl, and the size and shape of the bat-emblem,

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:50.470
is pretty much a direct mirror of Miller's
vision, and the surest giveaway for the Batfleck's

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:51.470
true inspiration.

00:01:51.470 --> 00:01:54.340
Well, that, and the fact that he basically
said it in an interview.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:57.680
"Zack was heavily influenced by that, I think
everyone's heavily influenced by that, that's

00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:58.680
the seminal work.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:02.159
So, I don't think there's any of these that
have been made that haven't drawn from that

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:04.649
book, because it's so good."

00:02:04.649 --> 00:02:05.649
Wonder Woman

00:02:05.649 --> 00:02:10.700
Lynda Carter's version of Wonder Woman from
the '70s TV show established a very literal

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:13.090
rendition of the iconic DC character.

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:17.540
But when Gal Gadot re-introduced Wonder Woman
in Batman v Superman: Dawn of Justice, she

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.319
did it in an updated outfit.

00:02:19.319 --> 00:02:23.680
Instead of the red, yellow, and blue primary
color scheme she typically wears in the comics,

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:28.000
she dons metallic body armor that fits in
nicely beside the new, grittier versions of

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:29.280
Batman and Superman.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:33.820
Based on that and what she's worn in footage
from 2017's Wonder Woman feature, it seems

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:38.440
that the amazing Amazon's classic outfit has
been translated nearly perfectly to the big

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:39.440
screen.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:40.440
Aquaman

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:44.180
While other superheroes have a long history
of on-screen adaptations, Aquaman has only

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:48.690
appeared on television a handful of times...and
all of them favored his usual orange and green

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:49.690
style.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:53.629
Instead, actor Jason Momoa seems to borrow
more from Aquaman’s later, more grizzled

00:02:53.629 --> 00:02:56.510
look, wearing a costume that highlights his
incredible brawn.

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:58.840
It's basically Poseidon meets Khal Drogo.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:02:59.840
And that's awesome.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.960
Still, this version of Aquaman will always
be closest to our hearts.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:07.940
(weird Aquaman sounds)

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:11.420
"Oh, hello old chum."

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:12.610
Green Arrow

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:16.330
Green Arrow's costume on television has gone
through some tweaks, but none have quite lined

00:03:16.330 --> 00:03:19.349
up with the Emerald Archer's longtime look
from the comics.

00:03:19.349 --> 00:03:24.230
While the original is a not-so-subtle tribute
to Robin Hood, Stephen Amell looks far darker

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:26.060
and more leathery in the new suit.

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:30.290
Additionally, for Arrow's first season, Ollie
didn't even bother wearing anything to cover

00:03:30.290 --> 00:03:32.989
his face, opting instead for black greasepaint.

00:03:32.989 --> 00:03:37.040
Eventually he wised up and went with a mask,
while keeping the practical and modernized

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:39.340
hood to go with the show's urban setting.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:40.340
The Flash

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:44.460
What happens when you have a TV show about
a superhero, but you want to include the same

00:03:44.460 --> 00:03:47.090
character in your movies without impacting
either one?

00:03:47.090 --> 00:03:51.430
You end up with Grant Gustin and Ezra Miller
both playing a dude named Barry Allen, the

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.180
Flash, the fastest man alive.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:55.370
Well, the fastest fictional man alive, at
least.

00:03:55.370 --> 00:04:00.131
The costume from the CW's TV series is mostly
faithful to the comic book original, but it's

00:04:00.131 --> 00:04:04.239
a deeper shade of red and has some unique
style choices: there's the lightning bolt

00:04:04.239 --> 00:04:08.730
seams in the headpiece that reflects his New
52 comic counterpart, the fact that his boots

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:12.530
are red instead of the signature yellow, and
the the lightning trimmings from his waist

00:04:12.530 --> 00:04:15.330
and forearms have been reduced and removed,
respectively.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:16.400
"I'm the Flash"

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.019
"The who now?"

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:22.449
Ezra Miller's movie Flash is even more distinct,
looking almost like he’s wearing armor.

00:04:22.449 --> 00:04:26.370
The inspiration for Miller's movie Flash,
it seems, is the character's look in the Injustice

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:27.590
video game series.

00:04:31.250 --> 00:04:35.810
In fact, Gustin has declared himself a fan
of the new Flash aesthetic, calling it "sick"

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:39.430
and adding that he's "glad it's really different"
and takes a different approach than the TV

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:40.930
show's "street, vintage feel."

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:44.900
Hopefully one day they'll actually meet, and
we'll get a true "Flash of Two Worlds" style

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:45.900
crossover.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.650
Nerds can dream, can't they?

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:49.400
Kid Flash

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.190
Kid Flash's costume got a few alterations
in The Flash TV series, too.

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:57.940
The costume worn by TV's Wally West has a
mustardy hue rather than a primary yellow,

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:01.470
and sewn-in gloves, as opposed to the separate
pieces in the comics.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:05.100
Meanwhile, his belt is thicker and distinct,
with a deeper shade of red.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.870
Of course, one of the most significant and
interesting changes is that this version of

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:13.350
Kid Flash is African-American, a new spin
on a character who for years in the comics

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:14.350
was white.

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:18.550
Because comics are endlessly confusing, there
is a comic book version of Kid Flash who reflects

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:22.350
this new interpretation...but no matter who
you think of as Wally West, that's still a

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:24.610
pretty darn accurate costume.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:25.870
The Atom

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:29.680
While there are some nods to the old comics
rendition of the Atom, the character's on-screen

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:34.180
adaptation seems to have included a full-on
molecular transformation from his original

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:35.180
appearance.

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:40.060
Brandon Routh's outfit incorporates pieces
of Atom's upgrade from DC Comics' New 52 reboot,

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:42.880
like his exosuit and armored helmet in place
of his mask.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:47.340
Andy Poon, the concept artist who created
the new costume, wrote that the decision to

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:51.470
replace the character's signature spandex
suit was for practical reasons, but he still

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:55.470
"tried to emulate the feel of the traditional
blue and red pattern as much as possible while

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:57.979
keeping it more on the grounded sci-fi armor."

00:05:57.979 --> 00:06:00.160
Martian Manhunter

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:04.170
The Manhunter from Mars, alternately known
as J'onn J'onzz, has been through a lot of

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:09.910
changes over his many eras of fictional existence,
and his live-action adaptation in TV's Supergirl

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:12.280
adds to the character's evolutionary history.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:16.310
On the show, David Harewood's makeup-enhanced
look for Martian Manhunter draws from bits

00:06:16.310 --> 00:06:20.960
and pieces of his comic past—the shape of
his head mirrors the New 52 style, while his

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:25.229
cross-shaped red chest design seems like more
of a nod to the style he's rocked for years.

00:06:25.229 --> 00:06:29.870
Meanwhile, the shape of his eyes and the spherical
orbs on his costume are callbacks to the Martian's

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:34.860
2006 standalone miniseries, while the costume
itself seems like a militarized version of

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.070
what the character was drawn with in the cartoon
Justice League: Doom.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:42.410
Even so, the Martian's TV version stands on
its own.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:43.410
Supergirl

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:47.480
If you're a superhero, chances are good you've
gone through a few significant costume changes

00:06:47.480 --> 00:06:48.480
here and there.

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:50.289
And that's more true for Supergirl than most.

00:06:50.289 --> 00:06:53.820
So when Warner Brothers decided to bring the
Girl of Steel to the small screen, they had

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:58.330
plenty of outfits to choose from—which showrunners
poked fun at during the show's pilot episode:

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:01.289
"I'm not flying around saving people in this
thing.

00:07:01.289 --> 00:07:03.449
I wouldn't wear this to the beach.

00:07:03.449 --> 00:07:04.800
Where's my cape?"

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:06.150
"Cape's are lame!"

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:09.870
Fortunately, good sense prevailed and Melissa
Benoist's Supergirl wears what's probably

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:12.039
the character's best version of the outfit.

00:07:12.039 --> 00:07:16.260
Even with the textured fabric that's become
a staple of live-action superheroics, the

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:20.400
Supergirl costume has all the most important
elements, from the bold blue top to the bright

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.580
red boots, skirt, and cape.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:25.560
It's indisputable: Supergirl's definitely
the best-dressed hero on television.

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:27.420
"Nice to meet you by the way."

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:28.750
"Nice to meet you too!"

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:30.020
"I really like your outfit."

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:31.090
"I like your glasses."

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:32.090
"Thanks!"

00:07:32.090 --> 00:07:34.360
Wish we could say the same thing for the Red
Tornado.

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:35.360
Yikes.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:37.540
Thanks for watching!

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:40.940
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:44.930
And leave us a comment to let us know which
live-action DC heroes you think look the best.

